=== Module 29 - LL(k), LL(*), and Backtracking RD ===

### PART 1 - one token isn't always enough

########## fixtures/expr.grammar
  E -> T Ep
  Ep -> + T Ep
  Ep -> epsilon
  T -> F Tp
  Tp -> * F Tp
  Tp -> epsilon
  F -> ( E )
  F -> id
  F -> num
  FIRST_1 per production:
    E -> T Ep              { ( , id , num }
    Ep -> + T Ep           { + }
    Ep -> epsilon          { () }
    T -> F Tp              { ( , id , num }
    Tp -> * F Tp           { * }
    Tp -> epsilon          { () }
    F -> ( E )             { ( }
    F -> id                { id }
    F -> num               { num }
  FIRST_2 per production:
    E -> T Ep              { ( ( , ( id , ( num , id , id * , id + , num , num * , num + }
    Ep -> + T Ep           { + ( , + id , + num }
    Ep -> epsilon          { () }
    T -> F Tp              { ( ( , ( id , ( num , id , id * , num , num * }
    Tp -> * F Tp           { * ( , * id , * num }
    Tp -> epsilon          { () }
    F -> ( E )             { ( ( , ( id , ( num }
    F -> id                { id }
    F -> num               { num }
  minimal k: 1

########## fixtures/label.grammar
  stmt -> label colon stmt
  stmt -> expr semi
  label -> id
  expr -> id
  expr -> id lparen rparen
  FIRST_1 per production:
    stmt -> label colon stmt { id }
    stmt -> expr semi      { id }
    label -> id            { id }
    expr -> id             { id }
    expr -> id lparen rparen { id }
  FIRST_2 per production:
    stmt -> label colon stmt { id colon }
    stmt -> expr semi      { id lparen , id semi }
    label -> id            { id }
    expr -> id             { id }
    expr -> id lparen rparen { id lparen }
  minimal k: 2

########## fixtures/equiv.grammar
  A -> x B
  A -> x C
  A -> y
  B -> A
  C -> A
  FIRST_1 per production:
    A -> x B               { x }
    A -> x C               { x }
    A -> y                 { y }
    B -> A                 { x , y }
    C -> A                 { x , y }
  FIRST_2 per production:
    A -> x B               { x x , x y }
    A -> x C               { x x , x y }
    A -> y                 { y }
    B -> A                 { x x , x y , y }
    C -> A                 { x x , x y , y }
  minimal k: none in 1..4 (grammar is ambiguous / not LL(k))

### PART 2 - backtracking recursive descent: the cost of no lookahead

equiv.grammar  (A -> x B | x C | y ;  B -> A ;  C -> A)  on  x^n y :
    n      parse trees    production tries
    1      2              11              
    2      4              27              
    3      8              59              
    4      16             123             
    5      32             251             
    6      64             507             
    7      128            1019            
    parse trees double each step (2^n) and so does the work -- no finite k helps.
    packrat parsing (Appendix X2) memoizes (nonterminal, position) -> linear.

expr.grammar  (LL(1))  on  id (+ id)^n :
    tokens parse trees    production tries
    1      1              9               
    3      1              17              
    5      1              25              
    7      1              33              
    9      1              41              
    11     1              49              
    13     1              57              
    exactly one parse tree; work grows LINEARLY -- disjoint FIRST sets mean each
    nonterminal's wrong alternatives fail on the first token.

### LL(*) in one line
ANTLR's adaptive LL(*): instead of a fixed k, build a small DFA over the
lookahead that reads as many tokens as needed to pick an alternative --
regular (not bounded) lookahead. Falls back to backtracking only when the
DFA can't decide. For label.grammar the DFA reads id then peeks one more:
  'colon' -> alternative 1 (label) ;  otherwise -> alternative 2 (expr).
